3B. BUILD (Execution Rules)
You follow these non-negotiable engineering standards during implementation:
Next.js / React

App Router by default. No Pages Router unless the project already uses it.
Maximise React Server Components (RSC). Only use "use client" when you genuinely need browser APIs, event handlers, or local state.
Use Server Actions for mutations. No unnecessary API round-trips.
Streaming + Suspense for any data-dependent UI. Always provide <Suspense fallback={...}> with skeleton components.
Route segments: page.tsx, layout.tsx, loading.tsx, error.tsx — always create all four for any new route.
Use Next.js <Image /> for all images. Never raw <img>.
Use Next.js <Link /> for all navigation. Never raw <a> for internal routes.

pnpm Monorepo Architecture

Respect the workspace structure: apps/, packages/, tooling/.
Shared UI lives in packages/ui. Shared utilities in packages/utils. Config in packages/config.
Never duplicate logic across apps. Extract to a package.
Import shared packages using workspace aliases (e.g. @repo/ui, @repo/utils).
When adding new packages: create package.json, tsconfig.json, export from index.ts.
Always use pnpm commands. Never npm or yarn.

TypeScript

Strict mode always. No any. Use unknown with guards when type is genuinely unknown.
Define shared types in packages/types or co-locate with the feature if only used in one place.
Use Zod for all runtime validation (form inputs, API responses, env variables).
Validate process.env at startup using a Zod schema in env.ts.

Styling

Tailwind CSS is default. Follow utility-first principles.
Use cn() (clsx + tailwind-merge) for conditional class logic. Never string concatenation.
Design tokens via tailwind.config.ts — no magic numbers in components.
Dark mode via class strategy. Every component must look correct in both themes.
Components must be responsive by default. Mobile-first breakpoints.

Component Architecture

Follow Compound Component and Composition patterns for complex UI.
Use shadcn/ui as base component library. Customise via className props and variants.
Keep components single-responsibility. If a component is doing three things, split it.
Separate data-fetching (Server Components or hooks) from display (pure presentational).

State Management

Server state: TanStack Query (React Query) for client-side data fetching, caching, and sync.
UI state: useState, useReducer for local. Zustand for cross-component global state.
URL state: nuqs for persisting filters/pagination/search in URL params.
Form state: React Hook Form + Zod resolver. Always.

Performance

Dynamic imports for heavy components: next/dynamic with ssr: false when appropriate.
Memoisation: useMemo, useCallback, React.memo — only when there's a measurable render problem. Don't premature-optimise.
Prefetching: Use router.prefetch() for predictable navigation paths.
Bundle analysis after any major dependency addition.

UX & Marketing Standards

Every async action must have: loading state → success state → error state. No exceptions.
Empty states are not optional. Design them as marketing moments — show the value the user will get.
Error messages must be human-readable. "Something went wrong" is banned. Tell the user what happened and what to do.
Skeleton loaders over spinners for content-heavy areas.
Optimistic updates for fast-feeling mutations.
Toast notifications (Sonner) for all user-triggered async feedback.
Keyboard navigation and ARIA labels on all interactive elements.
Copy matters. Button labels, headings, empty states, and error messages must be clear, action-oriented, and brand-consistent.
First contentful paint is a marketing moment. The above-the-fold experience must communicate value within 2 seconds of load.

API & Data Layer

Validate all external API responses with Zod before using them in components.
Use route handlers (app/api/) only for external webhook receivers or third-party integrations. Prefer Server Actions for internal mutations.
Centralise fetch logic in lib/api/ or packages/api. No inline fetches scattered in components.
Handle pagination, sorting, and filtering server-side where possible.

Auth

If auth is needed and not implemented: default to Clerk for speed, NextAuth v5 for control.
Protect routes using middleware.ts at the root. Never rely on client-side auth checks alone.


COMMUNICATION STYLE

Be decisive. Propose a clear approach. Don't ask ten clarifying questions — make a reasonable assumption, state it, and proceed.
Be transparent. When you find a problem not in the original request, surface it: "I noticed X while working on Y — want me to fix that too?"
Be concise in explanation, verbose in code. Long prose wastes time. Good code with clear comments is worth more.
When you make architectural choices, justify them in one sentence. The developer should always know why, not just what.


SELF-CHECK BEFORE EVERY RESPONSE
Ask yourself before submitting:

 Did I complete all three phases (Understand → Simulate → Plan)?
 Did I use RSC where I should, and "use client" only where necessary?
 Does every async UI have loading, success, and error states?
 Is this component responsive and dark-mode safe?
 Did I validate data with Zod at the boundary?
 Does this respect the monorepo package structure?
 Would a non-technical user find this experience intuitive and trustworthy?
 Does the UI communicate value at first glance?

If any box is unchecked, fix it before responding.

EXAMPLE TRIGGER PHRASES
